WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:03.860
Speaker 1: . It's my pleasure to welcome to
the program today, Joseph Westfall, who's

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:08.140
a senior fellow at the Wharton Leadership
Program at the University of Pennsylvania,

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:11.300
also a former U.S. ambassador to Saudi Arabia.

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:12.830
I really appreciate your time today.

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:15.320
Speaker 5: Yeah, David, glad to be with you.

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:19.289
By the way, I'm a professor at the Lawter
Institute at Wharton.

00:00:19.289 --> 00:00:25.449
Speaker 1: OK, an important, important detail
Speaker 5: to get credit for that.

00:00:25.449 --> 00:00:30.369
Speaker 1: So when it comes to you know, we've
talked for a long time about the U.S. relationship

00:00:30.369 --> 00:00:32.309
with Saudi Arabia.

00:00:32.309 --> 00:00:37.170
And I think that there is sometimes a simple
understanding, which is there's there's a

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:42.420
goal for shared stability in the region, often
in connection with with oil prices and the

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:43.960
oil industry.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:48.670
Is it quite that simple or how would you explain
the history of the relationship?

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:53.269
Speaker 5: No, I think it's it's a lot more
complicated than that.

00:00:53.269 --> 00:01:00.230
And, you know, the the one thing you have
to remember about Saudi Arabia is that U.S.

00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:06.490
public opinion, that is the average American
in Gallup polls after Gallup polls from the

00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:15.240
nineteen thirties to today to the latest polls
see Saudi Arabia in.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:21.120
Unfavorable terms, a majority of Americans
think of Saudi Arabia in unfavorable ways,

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:25.329
yes, in that regard, back to the attitude
that members of Congress then have to have

00:01:25.329 --> 00:01:27.360
because they get pressure from constituents.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:32.170
Some of that is because there's no knowledge
that Saudi Arabia was closed until recently

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:36.189
to the world for Americans having traveled
there, they haven't seen the country they

00:01:36.189 --> 00:01:41.500
haven't visited, although many Saudis have
come to the United States to study and to

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:43.009
work here.

00:01:43.009 --> 00:01:48.080
So I think that that that lack of knowledge
and that unfavorable rating play a big role.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:52.399
The other thing is, of course, that it is
the birthplace of Islam.

00:01:52.399 --> 00:02:00.030
So you cannot ignore a country that, you know,
hosts millions of people every year for religious

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:05.380
services, and it has the kind of influence
that they have around the world and have had

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:13.620
for a long time, in addition to the fact that
the region will not just for oil, but for

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:23.730
trade and for also for big nation, powerful
nations, China, Russia and others has become

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:27.630
a very important region where we're all competing.

00:02:27.630 --> 00:02:32.690
Speaker 1: Over the last I don't know how
many years I mean, we can we can talk about

00:02:32.690 --> 00:02:38.160
the last five years, but it's really sort
of been been seeded earlier than that.

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:43.890
There has been much made publicly of what
seems to be a kind of liberalization in some

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:50.780
sense in Saudi Arabia, arguably, at least
to some degree, because of the direction that

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:53.890
Mohammed bin Salman wants to see the country
go.

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:59.610
But at the same time, while publicly we're
hearing about women can now drive and some

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:06.240
of these other things we know about, you know,
the sequestering of dozens of officials in

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:11.730
a hotel in order to interrogate and in some
time sometimes make threats and even much,

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:12.940
much worse.

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:19.590
So as from a diplomatic perspective, when
one thing is happening publicly and yet very

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:24.660
different things are happening privately,
what are the conversations like between American

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:26.830
diplomats and the leaders of the country?

00:03:26.830 --> 00:03:30.230
Do they do they talk about what's happening
behind the scenes as much as they talk about

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:31.560
what's happening publicly?

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:37.120
Speaker 5: Well, it depends on which diplomat
you're talking about.

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:38.120
OK?

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:42.180
And it's really, really good question, because
diplomacy really changed dramatically after

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:44.480
the Obama administration.

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:51.100
So when when I left in twenty seventeen in
January, at the end of the Obama administration,

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:56.450
none of the things you pointed out had happened.

00:03:56.450 --> 00:03:57.570
Hmm.

00:03:57.570 --> 00:04:01.910
Mohammed bin Nayef was still the crown prince.

00:04:01.910 --> 00:04:09.870
I had excellent meetings with him, the king
and Mohammed bin Salman upon leaving.

00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:11.430
We had had a great relationship.

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:17.229
We worked on everything from their economic
transformation plan, which members had put

00:04:17.229 --> 00:04:18.229
forth.

00:04:18.229 --> 00:04:21.310
We worked on security issues with Mohammed
bin Nayef.

00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:26.740
We had a great relationship, extensive relationship,
trying to deal with all the regional issues

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:30.879
in Syria, Iraq and, of course, Yemen.

00:04:30.879 --> 00:04:37.080
We were fully, fully engaged as an embassy
in almost every aspect of the relationship

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:45.439
or every aspect of their economy, education,
energy, you name it, transportation, et cetera.

00:04:45.439 --> 00:04:50.290
We were involved when I left at twenty seventeen.

00:04:50.290 --> 00:04:55.389
President Trump did not appoint an ambassador
for two years, right?

00:04:55.389 --> 00:05:01.539
And so the only diplomacy that was going on
was between Pompilio.

00:05:01.539 --> 00:05:10.039
Secretary Pompeo, who would occasionally travel
there, and Kushnir.

00:05:10.039 --> 00:05:15.840
So so there wasn't the embassy was not empowered
to work any of these issues and didn't have

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:24.080
access, and of course, after I left about
a month or two after I left, MBBS was able

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:26.030
to get his father to Kapos.

00:05:26.030 --> 00:05:32.860
NBN will have a bit naive, right, as crown
prince and he was named the crown prince.

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:36.240
And then all the things that you mentioned
started to happen.

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:41.960
When so I think to ask you a question briefly,
let me give you a chance to have some more

00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:45.240
diplomacy is critical, but you've got to be
engaged holistically.

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:50.319
Speaker 1: Now, when you were there, certainly
you were aware and there was this history

00:05:50.319 --> 00:05:55.020
of the public executions and so on and so
forth.

00:05:55.020 --> 00:06:01.111
Does that get swept under the rug during diplomatic
conversations about, for example, Yemen, or

00:06:01.111 --> 00:06:02.850
is that present in the room?

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:09.780
Speaker 5: It's it's it did not get swept
under by us from the president, from President

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:13.759
Obama to the secretary of State Kerry to myself.

00:06:13.759 --> 00:06:19.999
And I'll just use myself as an example of
some ambassadors in some countries, Germany,

00:06:19.999 --> 00:06:30.479
Switzerland, Sweden and others would publicly
criticize and publicly.

00:06:30.479 --> 00:06:35.710
You know, be very negative about some of these
practices early on when I first got I chose

00:06:35.710 --> 00:06:36.710
a different method.

00:06:36.710 --> 00:06:40.190
I chose the idea that we needed to to.

00:06:40.190 --> 00:06:46.029
Help them make the right decisions, but not
going public with them in order to have access

00:06:46.029 --> 00:06:51.479
and confidence from them in us that we weren't
going to.

00:06:51.479 --> 00:06:56.210
So I had the conversations with the king,
with the crown prince, with the deputy crown

00:06:56.210 --> 00:07:04.569
prince, about lashings, about beheadings,
about security, executions, public executions,

00:07:04.569 --> 00:07:10.360
about the role of the Mutawa, the religious
police.

00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:16.930
Who are increasingly becoming a real, real
problem for them and especially for everybody

00:07:16.930 --> 00:07:22.740
else, and I was afraid that they were going
to start perhaps at some point attacking our

00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:24.509
foreign diplomats.

00:07:24.509 --> 00:07:28.099
So I was very straight with them that we weren't
going to tolerate any of this stuff.

00:07:28.099 --> 00:07:33.509
It had to change and it had to change because
our relationship was so important.

00:07:33.509 --> 00:07:40.020
Speaker 1: Now, how do they react, I mean,
do they do they write it off as well, you

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:43.099
know, that we just do things differently here
than you do in the United States?

00:07:43.099 --> 00:07:46.569
Or do they are they aware of how those practices
are seen around the world?

00:07:46.569 --> 00:07:48.970
Speaker 5: Yes, yes, absolutely.

00:07:48.970 --> 00:07:59.669
They do, especially namby pamby and B.S. is
was then very keen on knowing what the foreign

00:07:59.669 --> 00:08:03.889
press was saying about Saudi Arabia, about
him, about their practices.

00:08:03.889 --> 00:08:12.110
So they were very well aware of that and very
well aware that that our ability to support

00:08:12.110 --> 00:08:20.219
his plans for the economy, for transformation,
for modernizing the country depended on eliminating

00:08:20.219 --> 00:08:25.129
those those ancient practices that that he
didn't agree with.

00:08:25.129 --> 00:08:26.460
Right.

00:08:26.460 --> 00:08:31.770
And we're tolerated because it was still a
very conservative element within Saudi Arabia.

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:37.010
People who, you know, I don't know if they
demanded that, but they expected that.

00:08:37.010 --> 00:08:44.670
So when I went to talk to them about it, they
got it and they took action and they eliminated

00:08:44.670 --> 00:08:45.680
all those things.

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:50.850
But I went back to the to the to their precincts.

00:08:50.850 --> 00:08:54.720
The the lashings were terminated.

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:59.550
And the president was saying the same thing
in meetings with the king whenever we come.

00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:01.530
He did four trips to Saudi Arabia.

00:09:01.530 --> 00:09:04.070
I was president, three of them.

00:09:04.070 --> 00:09:05.600
One was before I became a guest.

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:09.350
Speaker 1: Now, for much of what I've read,
it's unclear.

00:09:09.350 --> 00:09:13.760
Well, I'll put it a different way from what
I've read.

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:21.480
It's not clear to me whether the reasons that
Mohammed bin Salman is taking these positions

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:25.810
of, you know, the reforms that you talked
about in that I talked about, if it's because

00:09:25.810 --> 00:09:33.210
this is really what he believes, or rather,
it's because they've seemed to recognize that

00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:39.140
the oil industry may not be the best place
to be solely focused in the future.

00:09:39.140 --> 00:09:44.110
There's this idea of diversifying the Saudi
sovereign wealth fund.

00:09:44.110 --> 00:09:49.390
They need to be playing nice with London and
New York and so on and so forth, and that

00:09:49.390 --> 00:09:53.630
it's really, you know, just just more of a
strategic and financial decision.

00:09:53.630 --> 00:09:58.280
Like what makes you think that Mohammed bin
Salman really thinks these aren't good practices?

00:09:58.280 --> 00:10:05.150
Speaker 5: Well, you know, when he would again,
when I was ambassador, he took some trips

00:10:05.150 --> 00:10:06.320
to the United States.

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:07.850
Yeah, he did.

00:10:07.850 --> 00:10:09.560
Those trips, he met with everybody.

00:10:09.560 --> 00:10:10.820
He went to California.

00:10:10.820 --> 00:10:13.870
He met with the tech people.

00:10:13.870 --> 00:10:15.450
He met with Facebook people.

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:17.360
He met with Bloomberg.

00:10:17.360 --> 00:10:21.010
He he met with dozens of people across the
board.

00:10:21.010 --> 00:10:22.150
I got them.

00:10:22.150 --> 00:10:30.920
I wanted them to do their their big when their
big investment and through their IPO IPO,

00:10:30.920 --> 00:10:36.970
through the New York Stock Exchange, right
out of me at the New York Stock Exchange.

00:10:36.970 --> 00:10:44.390
And and, you know, it was clear that he was
trying to build support for his economic transformation

00:10:44.390 --> 00:10:45.740
plan.

00:10:45.740 --> 00:10:47.510
And he wanted the United States involved.

00:10:47.510 --> 00:10:49.660
He wanted the United States engaged.

00:10:49.660 --> 00:10:54.770
President Obama actually met with him and
had him over at the White House for a meeting.

00:10:54.770 --> 00:10:58.330
So things were where I thought things were
going very well.

00:10:58.330 --> 00:11:02.650
Speaker 1: And you thought it was a genuine
desire to reform, not just a financial incentive.

00:11:02.650 --> 00:11:09.230
Speaker 5: That was, you know, the only way
I started with him was when I first got there,

00:11:09.230 --> 00:11:12.270
the King Salman was the crown prince.

00:11:12.270 --> 00:11:13.270
Right.

00:11:13.270 --> 00:11:14.270
Right.

00:11:14.270 --> 00:11:16.160
Was Abdullah, who was also a great guy.

00:11:16.160 --> 00:11:18.220
And I really enjoyed working with him.

00:11:18.220 --> 00:11:22.060
But Mohammed miscued King Salman was the crown
prince.

00:11:22.060 --> 00:11:27.750
And he's the one that I principally worked
with because he was the son of the prime minister.

00:11:27.750 --> 00:11:35.480
And I noticed his son sitting in a couple
of meetings far off in the corner, not participating.

00:11:35.480 --> 00:11:39.760
And so I asked for a meeting with him and
we met.

00:11:39.760 --> 00:11:45.910
And from that first meeting where we just
got to know each other, I realized two things.

00:11:45.910 --> 00:11:51.890
One, this this young man is someday going
to be the king of Saudi.

00:11:51.890 --> 00:11:52.890
Hmm.

00:11:52.890 --> 00:11:53.890
Number one.

00:11:53.890 --> 00:12:00.800
Number two, because he's ambitious and and
he has always been interested in the political

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:03.760
and economic development of his country from
the time he was a young man.

00:12:03.760 --> 00:12:05.370
How do I know that?

00:12:05.370 --> 00:12:07.570
Because then it's a background work on him.

00:12:07.570 --> 00:12:13.710
So I saw it is his other brothers were not
necessarily of the same thing, but he was.

00:12:13.710 --> 00:12:19.350
And that's why his father loved him so much,
because his father was a consummate politician

00:12:19.350 --> 00:12:22.790
and his son was like him.

00:12:22.790 --> 00:12:26.870
And so we developed a great friendship and
the friendship was, you know, tell each other

00:12:26.870 --> 00:12:27.870
the truth.

00:12:27.870 --> 00:12:30.000
I will tell you exactly what I think.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:34.720
I represent the president and my country and
I will tell you exactly what I think.

00:12:34.720 --> 00:12:38.900
And I expect you to be polite back if you
don't agree with me.

00:12:38.900 --> 00:12:43.990
If you're not going to do what I ask you to
do, then just tell me and you will negotiate

00:12:43.990 --> 00:12:44.990
further.

00:12:44.990 --> 00:12:47.910
And that's the way we work for for the three
years I was there.

00:12:47.910 --> 00:12:54.470
Speaker 1: In this sort of limited time, we
have left the Jamal Khashoggi murder.

00:12:54.470 --> 00:13:01.230
I'm curious your thoughts on how the Trump
administration responded to that initially.

00:13:01.230 --> 00:13:06.150
Donald Trump kind of saying, I don't know,
I just spoke to the king and they say they

00:13:06.150 --> 00:13:07.150
weren't involved.

00:13:07.150 --> 00:13:08.150
I believe them.

00:13:08.150 --> 00:13:09.150
I don't know.

00:13:09.150 --> 00:13:10.150
Seems seems pretty reasonable.

00:13:10.150 --> 00:13:14.510
And then, of course, the more we learned about
it, the more we learned about how at the very

00:13:14.510 --> 00:13:18.230
close to the very top, if not at the very
top, they seem to have known exactly what

00:13:18.230 --> 00:13:19.230
was going on.

00:13:19.230 --> 00:13:21.110
How do you think that was handled by the Trump
administration?

00:13:21.110 --> 00:13:24.190
Speaker 5: I don't think it was handled well
at all.

00:13:24.190 --> 00:13:29.440
And I think that that in some ways they should
have done to some extent with President Biden

00:13:29.440 --> 00:13:33.550
is doing now saying, you know, we're not going
to we're not going to cut.

00:13:33.550 --> 00:13:41.150
We don't we can't prove without a without
a without any doubt that he ordered this.

00:13:41.150 --> 00:13:45.610
He has publicly said he didn't and that there's
a rogue operation.

00:13:45.610 --> 00:13:51.960
But whatever we know, whatever we understand
from it, we're going to have to recalibrate

00:13:51.960 --> 00:13:57.440
as the President Biden has put in this relationship.

00:13:57.440 --> 00:14:05.090
And he didn't do that, you know, I mean, again,
there was no real diplomacy going on, there

00:14:05.090 --> 00:14:09.610
was no effort to really end this war in Yemen
in an aggressive way.

00:14:09.610 --> 00:14:16.350
There was no effort to try to tone down the
ambitions of the crown prince.

00:14:16.350 --> 00:14:23.090
And his ambitions are some of them are personal
for his own power and influence.

00:14:23.090 --> 00:14:26.720
But many of them are also for his country.

00:14:26.720 --> 00:14:31.700
And we needed to be sure that that we can
offset the influence that other countries

00:14:31.700 --> 00:14:39.930
like China would begin to have, instead, Trump
was focusing only on the sale of military

00:14:39.930 --> 00:14:40.930
hardware.

00:14:40.930 --> 00:14:41.930
Yes.

00:14:41.930 --> 00:14:42.930
And.

00:14:42.930 --> 00:14:45.050
And I thought that was absolutely wrong.

00:14:45.050 --> 00:14:51.330
Speaker 1: We've been speaking with former
U.S. ambassador to Saudi Arabia, Arabia, Joseph

00:14:51.330 --> 00:14:52.380
Westfall.

00:14:52.380 --> 00:14:57.390
I so appreciate your time today and this will
certainly be an issue we will continue discussing.

00:14:57.390 --> 00:14:59.500
Speaker 5: Wonderful to be with you, David.

00:14:59.500 --> 00:15:00.310
Thank you very much.

